# build artifacts
/target

# agent related
.code-review-graph/
!.claude/settings.json
llms-full.txt

# temporary docs
PREPLAN.md

# poc -- committed and tagged `poc` (PLAN §6): a reference to diff against,
# never a foundation to inherit.
proof-of-concept/target/

# What `installua stubs` writes, run against this repo. Generated files with no
# reader -- the stubs tests assert against `stubs::meta()` rather than the file,
# so a committed copy is a snapshot nothing checks and everything outdates.
# Named individually because `.installua/headers/*.toml` is hand-written and
# belongs in a project's history.
.installua/meta/
.installua/installua.yml

# Assembled installers, written by examples/assemble.sh and cleaned up by it.
examples/**/*-setup.exe

# `emit` writes beside its input, so running it on an example lands an
# `install.nsi` next to the `install.lua` -- a byte-identical copy of the
# `generated.nsi` golden already committed there. One was, once. The goldens
# themselves are committed on purpose: tests/examples.rs diffs against them.
examples/*/install.nsi

# Tier 3 for the examples writes the script where the assets are and removes it
# again; this is what an interrupted run leaves behind.
examples/*/assembling.nsi

# Opaque payload fixtures, written by examples/fixtures.py. Nothing reads their
# bytes, so they are generated rather than vendored -- which also keeps NSIS's
# own artwork out of this repo and sidesteps a global `*.exe` ignore.
examples/*/assets/*.ico
examples/*/assets/*.exe

# Tier 3 for the overlay examples writes the script where the fixtures are and
# removes it again; these are what an interrupted run leaves behind. The
# fixtures themselves are committed -- unlike the examples' artwork, they are
# small, real files of their type and every test needs them present.
tests/fixtures/assembling.nsi
tests/fixtures/*.exe

# The same for tests/cli.rs, which writes a program where a relative path would
# resolve from, runs the binary against it and removes it again.
tests/fixtures/check-*.lua
tests/fixtures/emit-*.lua
tests/fixtures/check-*.nsi
tests/fixtures/emit-*.nsi

# `version.dll` is a fixture and not a build product: `GetDLLVersionLocal` needs
# a real PE carrying a version resource, and `make-version-dll.py` beside it is
# how this one was made. Named explicitly because a global excludes file that
# ignores `*.dll` is the normal setup, and a fixture that silently never gets
# committed is a repository that does not build for anyone else.
!tests/fixtures/assets/version.dll

# lua-language-server scratch (--logpath, --check_out_path)
verification/**/.out*

mise.*
rust-toolchain.toml
